From d88713eaada52f6c7ebd424be7e6cd588bf928bf Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: gaia <build@heygaia.io>
Date: Fri, 2 Oct 2026 01:11:28 +0530
Subject: [PATCH] fix(render): hit-test in paint order, so a click lands on
 what is drawn on top

element_at_point picked the box with the highest NodeId that contained the
point: a proxy for tree order that breaks for any node inserted later, and that
ignored z-index, fixed positioning, the CSS 2 painting layers and
pointer-events. A click aimed at an absolutely positioned menu item went to the
in-flow container painted under it, and focused that.

Paint order is now one function (stacking_paint_order) shared by paint and hit
testing. It also paints what CSS 2 Appendix E says: a stacking context's own
background under its negative z-index children; positioned boxes with
z-index:auto, and opacity/transform stacking contexts, in the z-index:0 layer
above floats and in-flow content; floats and z-index:auto positioned boxes as
pseudo stacking contexts whose positioned descendants belong to the enclosing
context; and in-flow inline text before the positioned layers rather than over
them. Hit testing walks that order backwards, through transforms (inverse
mapped) and overflow clips, and skips visibility:hidden and pointer-events:none
boxes (now parsed, inherited); opacity does not matter, as in Chrome.

elementsFromPoint returns every hit topmost first, ending at the root element,
and elementFromPoint is its first entry, so the two agree. Both retarget each
hit against the document or shadow root asking, so no shadow tree, open or
closed, leaks through them. __obscura_hitTarget gives input dispatch the real,
un-retargeted target.

